# Pipeline Steps

## Step 1 - Upload

A client uploads a document through the REST API.

Currently supported:

* PDF
* Plain Text

Future support:

* DOCX
* HTML
* Markdown
* Excel
* Images (OCR)

---

## Step 2 - Store Original File

The uploaded file is immediately stored.

Purpose:

* Preserve the original document.
* Allow future reprocessing.
* Avoid re-uploading files.

Current implementation:

```text id="fwgjl9"
Local File Storage
```

Future implementations may include:

* AWS S3
* Azure Blob Storage
* Google Cloud Storage
* MinIO

---

## Step 3 - Create Document Version

Every upload creates a new version.

Example:

```text id="nl0s8s"
Employee Handbook

Version 1

↓

Version 2

↓

Version 3
```

Only one version is active.

Previous versions remain available for history.

---

## Step 4 - Text Extraction

The processing service loads the stored file and extracts plain text.

Current implementation:

```text id="b4jlwm"
Apache PDFBox
```

Output:

```text id="n2qud6"
PDF

↓

Plain Text
```

The extracted text becomes the input for chunking.

---

## Step 5 - Chunking

Large documents are divided into smaller sections.

Example:

```text id="v3kbg0"
Document

↓

6,433 Characters

↓

13 Chunks
```

Current strategy:

* Character-based chunking
* Fixed chunk size
* Overlap between chunks

Reason:

Large Language Models perform significantly better when retrieving smaller, focused passages instead of entire documents.

Future chunking strategies:

* Token-based
* Sentence-based
* Paragraph-based
* Semantic chunking

---

## Step 6 - Metadata Generation

Each chunk stores metadata describing its origin.

Current metadata:

```json id="1q0qwc"
{}
```

Future metadata may include:

```json id="yqyyda"
{
  "page": 5,
  "section": "Leave Policy",
  "heading": "Annual Leave",
  "tokenCount": 230,
  "language": "en"
}
```

Metadata enables advanced filtering during retrieval.

Examples:

* Search only page 10
* Search HR section
* Search English documents

---

## Step 7 - Persist Chunks

Each chunk is stored in PostgreSQL.

Example:

```text id="n0jknl"
Document Version

↓

Chunk 1

Chunk 2

Chunk 3

...

Chunk N
```

Each chunk keeps:

* chunk number
* text
* metadata
* processing status

---

## Step 8 - Embedding Generation

Each chunk is converted into a semantic vector.

Current provider:

```text id="jlwmrb"
Ollama
```

Current model:

```text id="rqjz2o"
nomic-embed-text
```

Current vector size:

```text id="mjlwm9"
768 Dimensions
```

Example:

```text id="lzxjwr"
Chunk Text

↓

Embedding Generator

↓

768-Dimensional Vector
```

The application communicates with Ollama through an abstraction:

```text id="h6ylxo"
EmbeddingGenerator
        │
        ▼
OllamaEmbeddingGenerator
```

Future providers:

* OpenAI
* Gemini
* Azure OpenAI

can be added without changing the processing workflow.

---

## Step 9 - Persist Embeddings

Generated vectors are stored in PostgreSQL using pgvector.

Relationship:

```text id="bjlwm4"
Document Chunk

↓

Embedding

↓

Vector
```

Current database:

* PostgreSQL
* pgvector

This allows similarity search using vector distance.

---

## Step 10 - Version Activation

Once every chunk and embedding has been successfully stored:

1. Current version becomes **ACTIVE**.
2. Previous active version becomes **INACTIVE**.

Example:

```text id="i4r7y8"
Version 16

↓

INACTIVE

Version 17

↓

ACTIVE
```

This guarantees retrieval always searches the latest processed version.

---

# Error Handling

If any step fails:

```text id="tw0y3m"
Upload

↓

Processing

↓

Failure

↓

Version remains inactive
```

The previous active version continues serving search requests.

No partially processed version becomes active.

